M11 agent-loop design sign-off
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The M11 agent-loop follow-up (docs/m11-followups/01-agent-loop.md) adds an
LLM-driven plan→act→observe loop and is an AGENTS.md escalation trigger; its
design is reviewed and signed off before implementation.

Design: docs/m11-agent-loop-design.md (a DELTA on the reviewed tool-execution
sandbox — it changes who selects the tool, not how a tool runs). The loop
reuses, unchanged, the out-of-process SandboxExecutor, the deny-by-default
per-tenant allow-list (re-checked every call), the operator kill-switch
(AGENT_TOOLS_ENABLED — disabling tools disables the loop), and the shape-only
audit log. New controls: server-enforced step / wall-clock / token budgets
(not client- or model-settable), a model-agnostic JSON action protocol whose
output is treated as untrusted, and clean refusal (503) when inference is cold.

Sign-off: approved by the maintainer for this increment. The sandbox is NOT
relaxed for the loop; model output and tool observations cannot widen
authorization or budgets. Network-egress / FS-write / credential tools remain
EXCLUDED (require the Job sandbox, follow-up 3). The "deep-research" sub-feature
(follow-up 2) remains NOT adopted and keeps its separate decision record +
Apache-2.0 attribution checkpoint.

Provenance: no third-party agent framework is vendored; the loop is original
control-plane code over the existing inference client.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
M11 Job-sandbox design sign-off
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The M11 Job-sandbox follow-up (docs/m11-followups/03-job-sandbox.md) adds a
SECOND, stronger executor for IO-capable tools — a per-call Kubernetes Job — and
is an AGENTS.md escalation trigger (it introduces a component that can create
pods). Reviewed and signed off before implementation.

Design: docs/m11-job-sandbox-design.md (a DELTA on the reviewed subprocess
sandbox; the subprocess sandbox stays the default for pure-compute tools and is
NOT relaxed). An earlier draft that granted the control-plane ServiceAccount
Job-create RBAC was REJECTED at review for blast radius. The signed-off design
uses the TIGHTER privilege model: the internet-facing control plane keeps ZERO
Kubernetes privileges; Job creation is isolated in a small, non-user-facing
tool-runner dispatcher (its own `agent-jobs` namespace, ingress restricted to
the control-plane namespace + a shared token) that owns a FIXED, locked-down pod
template the control plane cannot influence. The runner Job is unprivileged: a
dedicated SA with NO IRSA and automountServiceAccountToken=false, runAsNonRoot,
read-only rootfs, dropped capabilities, default-deny NetworkPolicy (IMDS/egress
blocked), scoped emptyDir, activeDeadlineSeconds, backoffLimit=0, and
ttlSecondsAfterFinished.

Sign-off: approved for this increment with the tighter model. The first
Job-backed tool is a pure-compute demonstrator (no real network egress) so the
isolation mechanics can be validated without opening egress. Network-egress /
filesystem-writing-beyond-the-scoped-volume / credential-access tools remain
EXCLUDED by default and require, per tool, an egress allow-list and their own
review. The control plane gains NO Kubernetes RBAC.

Provenance: no third-party agent/runtime framework is vendored; the dispatcher
and its in-cluster Kubernetes client are original stdlib-only control-plane
code.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
M11 deep-research adoption decision
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The optional M11 "deep-research" sub-feature (docs/m11-followups/02-deep-research.md)
is a multi-step agent workflow (plan → retrieve → synthesize). The milestone
requires a separate adoption decision here, plus an Apache-2.0 attribution
checkpoint IF upstream code is adapted.

Decision: ADOPTED, implemented as ORIGINAL control-plane code. NO upstream
"deep-research" source is vendored or adapted — the controller is written from
scratch over the already-reviewed M11 agent loop (docs/m11-agent-loop-design.md)
and the existing M10 retrieval store. Therefore the Apache-2.0 attribution
checkpoint is N/A (nothing to attribute); no third-party agent/runtime framework
is introduced.

Constraints (carried, not relaxed): runs only when the tenant is allow-listed
for the "deep_research" capability (deny by default); shares the operator
kill-switch (AGENT_TOOLS_ENABLED) and refuses cleanly when inference is cold;
server-enforced budgets (sub-query count, top-k, wall-clock, tokens); retrieval
is the tenant's OWN corpus via the M10 store (per-tenant isolation, no bypass);
audit/telemetry/notifications carry shape + run id only — never the question,
retrieved passages, or the synthesized answer (M5 content policy).

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
M12 MCP adoption decision
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

M12 (docs/milestones/m12-mcp-integration.md) exposes capabilities over the Model
Context Protocol. Per the Phase 2 doc it is "standard" risk and depends on M11;
adopting any individual MCP server is a separate per-server escalation trigger.

Decision: the MCP integration LAYER is ADOPTED, implemented as ORIGINAL
control-plane code. The connection-manager is reused as a PATTERN only — no MCP
SDK or upstream connection-manager is vendored; the JSON-RPC stdio client is
written from scratch over the reviewed M11 sandbox (docs/m12-mcp-design.md). MCP
servers run as sandboxed subprocesses (the M11 boundary); connections are
per-call (no pooled cross-tenant sessions); access is deny-by-default per tenant;
credentials are resolved per-tenant from managed secret storage and injected only
into that one process (never shared env); audit is shape-only; an operator
kill-switch (MCP_ENABLED) disables it cluster-wide.

Per-server records: the ONLY server shipped in this increment is a pure STUB
(app/mcp_servers/stub_server.py) — stdlib-only, no network, no credentials, one
inert `echo` tool. Nothing to license-review or credential-scope. Any real MCP
server is a separate adoption decision with its own NOTICE record + licensing /
isolation review (and, if it needs egress/creds, the Job sandbox + issue #36).

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Vector-storage decision record (M3 deferral)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

M3 build task 4 requires a vector-storage strategy decision.

Decision: deferred to M4 (inference plane MVP).

Rationale:
- Vector retrieval is tightly coupled to the inference model and its embedding
  dimensionality; the vLLM integration (M4) must be in place before a
  meaningful retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) design can be validated.
- No production-critical M3 feature depends on vector storage.
- Introducing a vector database (pgvector, Qdrant, etc.) before the inference
  contract is validated would risk over-engineering or premature lock-in.

Escalation: the vector-storage strategy requires maintainer review before
adoption, per the M3 escalation triggers.  A recommendation will be brought
to review at the start of M4.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
M13 shared-harness adoption decision
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

M13 (docs/milestones/m13-personal-info-integrations.md) adds optional personal-
information integrations. This record covers the SHARED HARNESS only (milestone
build-tasks #2–#6); each real integration is a separate per-integration adoption
+ credential review. Design + escalation: docs/m13-shared-harness-escalation.md
(decisions A–C) and docs/m13-followups/00-build-plan.md.

Decision: the M13 shared harness is ADOPTED, implemented as ORIGINAL control-
plane code. No upstream integration code is vendored (the upstream CardDAV-class
URL/credential handling flagged in 02-review-summary.md is specifically NOT
ported).

- Outbound calls route through a new stdlib URL-validation layer
  (app/control_plane/outbound.py), designated the M3 hardened layer of record
  (Decision A): https-only, deny-by-default host allow-list, resolve-then-pin
  against DNS rebinding, private/loopback/link-local/reserved + the cloud
  metadata IPs (169.254.169.254 / fd00:ec2::254, incl. IPv4-mapped) blocked. A
  narrow opt-in permit_hosts waives ONLY the private-IP class for an explicitly
  trusted in-cluster host (the dev fixture); the metadata block is never waived.
- Per-tenant credentials are resolved at runtime from AWS Secrets Manager via
  IRSA (app/control_plane/integration_secrets.py; boto3 behind an injected
  interface, already present via app/storage/s3.py), scoped to
  <project>/<env>/integrations/<tenant>/<integration>[/<user>] — the IRSA grant
  is prefix-scoped to .../integrations/* (Decision B), no blanket secret access.
  Production resolves ONLY through Secrets Manager; a development-only fixture
  token path is gated to ENVIRONMENT=development.
- Access is deny-by-default per tenant (INTEGRATIONS_ALLOWLIST); an operator
  kill-switch (INTEGRATIONS_ENABLED) disables it cluster-wide; a per-tenant DB
  switch (integration_tenant_state) disables it per tenant. Audit is shape-only
  (host, method, response class, latency, decision, reject-reason — never URL
  path/query, params, or credentials). A dedicated rate/concurrency limiter is
  used (not the agent-tools budget).

Per-integration records: the harness increment shipped a synthetic LOOPBACK
FIXTURE (app/integration_fixtures/, fake in-cluster calendar server) plus a
blocked_probe used solely to dev-validate the harness — no real provider, no real
credentials. The harness was validated end-to-end on the dev cluster against the
fixture over the REAL Secrets Manager/IRSA path (per-tenant credential resolution,
SSRF block, cross-tenant denial, credential rotation without pod restart, and the
per-tenant kill-switch). Any real integration (calendar/contacts/mail) is a
separate adoption decision (Decision C) with its own NOTICE record, SDK licensing
review, and credential-handling security review (milestone task #6).

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
M13 Google Calendar integration (first real provider)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The first REAL personal-information integration adopted on the M13 harness
(Decision C). Implemented as ORIGINAL control-plane code
(app/control_plane/integrations_google.py).

Decision: ADOPTED (read-only calendar access: list events, get event).

- Licensing: NO SDK vendored. Requests are plain HTTPS against the public Google
  Calendar REST API (www.googleapis.com), so there is no third-party
  dependency/licensing footprint to review beyond the existing stdlib + the
  harness. Google API Terms of Service govern use of the API itself.
- Credential model: per-tenant (per-user where configured) OAuth2 credentials are
  resolved at request time from AWS Secrets Manager via IRSA, identical to every
  integration — never env vars/ConfigMaps. The stored secret holds CLIENT_ID /
  CLIENT_SECRET / REFRESH_TOKEN; the harness exchanges them for a short-lived
  access token at oauth2.googleapis.com/token (a guard-routed outbound call) and
  caches it per (tenant, integration) for the lifetime Google reports, refreshing
  before expiry. The minted access token is injected as a bearer and is never
  logged or stored. (The refresh capability is a generic harness feature —
  integrations.TokenRefresh — not Google-specific.)
- Security: the integration is a pure request builder and opens no socket; all
  egress routes through the hardened URL guard. permit_private_hosts is EMPTY
  (unlike the dev fixture), so the full guard applies — only the public
  www.googleapis.com host is reachable; private/loopback/metadata targets are
  refused (covered by a defense-in-depth rebinding test). Access is deny-by-
  default per tenant; the operator + per-tenant kill-switches apply.
- Live validation against the real Google API requires real OAuth2 credentials
  provisioned per tenant in Secrets Manager; that provisioning is a maintainer
  step (real third-party endpoints are out of scope for the dev fixture). The
  integration + credential path + guard routing are unit-covered
  (tests/test_google_calendar.py).

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
M14 media-services adoption decision
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

M14 (docs/milestones/m14-media-services.md) adds optional GPU media services. This
record covers the SHARED HARNESS (control-plane routing + the generic media-service
Helm chart); each model is a separate per-service adoption with its own license
record below.

Decision: the M14 media harness is ADOPTED, implemented as ORIGINAL control-plane
code (app/control_plane/media.py) + an ORIGINAL generic Helm chart
(deploy/helm/media-service, vLLM-shape). No model weights are vendored into any
image — they are pulled at runtime. Each media service runs as its own isolated
GPU release: ClusterIP, NetworkPolicy restricting ingress to the control-plane
namespace, GPU taint toleration + inference-plane nodeSelector. The control plane
is the only caller and enforces: deny-by-default per-tenant allow-list
(MEDIA_ALLOWLIST), operator kill-switch (MEDIA_ENABLED), per-tenant disable
(reuses integration_tenant_state), a dedicated per-tenant rate/concurrency limiter,
server-side size caps (audio bytes / prompt chars), per-tenant S3 isolation for
artifacts (key media/<tenant>/<user>/...), shape-only audit (never media content/
prompts/transcripts), and media_task_completed/failed notifications. Backends are
reached over plain in-cluster HTTP (trusted internal services, like vLLM) — NOT
the M13 integration egress path.

Per-model records:

  M14 Whisper STT (first model). Decision: ADOPTED. Model = OpenAI Whisper
  (Systran/faster-whisper-small for dev) — weights are MIT, UNGATED (no HF token).
  Runtime = faster-whisper / CTranslate2 (MIT) via an OpenAI-compatible server
  image (fedirz/faster-whisper-server, MIT; pin a digest + re-review before any
  production use). No copyleft/AGPL in the default dependency set. Exposes
  /v1/audio/transcriptions. Live GPU validation uses the smallest model to bound
  cost; the routing/limits/isolation are unit-covered (tests/test_media.py).

  M14 SDXL image-gen (second model). Decision: ADOPTED, conditional on per-deploy
  license acceptance. Model = Stable Diffusion XL base 1.0
  (stabilityai/stable-diffusion-xl-base-1.0). License = CreativeML Open RAIL++-M —
  NOT permissive: it carries use-restrictions (the RAIL "Attachment A" prohibited
  uses) that must be reviewed and accepted before any deployment; this is the
  media-licensing risk flagged in docs/12-phase-2-feature-adoption.md. Weights are
  publicly downloadable (no HF token). The serving runtime (e.g. diffusers,
  Apache-2.0) and the concrete server image are NOT pinned here — there is no
  canonical OpenAI-compatible SDXL image, so deploy/values/dev/media-sdxl.yaml
  leaves image.repository empty: a license-reviewed, digest-pinned image exposing
  /v1/images/generations must be supplied before deploying, and re-reviewed for
  its dependency licensing (no AGPL in the default set). The control-plane
  generate path (per-tenant S3 isolation, size/content caps, deny-by-default) is
  unit-covered (tests/test_media.py); live GPU validation of SDXL is deferred (the
  dev media validation uses the smaller Whisper model per the M14 cost
  discipline).
